# Commands run by the launchd job com.redwood.demo (see com.redwood.demo.plist).
# RC_JOBS=rc.jobs.net19, WIFID=1, connsys_step=17, XNU_SECS=130: the radio's power, and traffic with it
# gone and back, with wifid given back afterwards.
#
# Run 86 (rc.jobs.net18) showed the off half in the kernel's own words -- "the link is down", "the
# station says: power, 0", no address on en0, reach NOT reachable, the fetch timed out -- and the on
# half as far as the radio goes: "joining", "authentication accepted", "association accepted", "EAPOL
# ... the keys go in", "the link is up" in 237 ms. What did not come back was the address: with wifid
# still frozen, configd's IPConfiguration said "done" but sent no DHCP packet at all.
#
# This run gives wifid back before expecting the address (it is what iOS's Wi-Fi side, and apparently
# the service behind configd, waits for). It also asks whether the address returns by itself ("waitaddr"
# straight after the join) before asking for it.
#
# The kernel now clears IFF_RUNNING when the link goes down, so "show" should say "up" and not "up
# running" while the radio is off.
/redwood/bin/net_test wait 22
/redwood/bin/net_test dhcp en0 waitaddr en0 15
/redwood/bin/net_test show
/redwood/bin/net_test fetch https://example.com/ 20
/redwood/bin/net_test stop wifid
/redwood/bin/net_test radio off
/redwood/bin/net_test wait 3
/redwood/bin/net_test show
/redwood/bin/net_test reach example.com
/redwood/bin/net_test fetch http://example.com/ 8
/redwood/bin/net_test radio on
/redwood/bin/net_test cont wifid
/redwood/bin/net_test wait 5
/redwood/bin/net_test waitaddr en0 10
/redwood/bin/net_test show
/redwood/bin/net_test dhcp en0 waitaddr en0 10
/redwood/bin/net_test fetch https://example.com/ 30
/redwood/bin/net_test end